<section class="solution" aria-labelledby="solution-title">
  <div class="section-inner">
    <h2 id="solution-title">Evidence first, then your approval</h2>
    <p class="lead">
      factory-kit is a thin layer over tools you already run. Hermes Kanban
      schedules the work, IDD skills do the engineering and GitHub stays the
      source of truth. factory-kit adds the checks between them.
    </p>
    <div class="feature-grid">
      <article class="feature-card">
        <h3>Reviews tied to one SHA.</h3>
        <p>
          A separate reviewer session records its verdict against the exact
          commit, and your CI has to pass on that same head.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <h3>Previews of the reviewed revision.</h3>
        <p>
          factory-kit publishes that exact commit as a preview on GitHub Pages
          or Vercel and smoke-tests the URL before it asks you anything.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <h3>Approvals that expire.</h3>
        <p>
          Your approval covers one head, one base and one preview for 60
          minutes. A new push cancels it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="feature-card">
        <h3>Recovery without duplicates.</h3>
        <p>
          Durable state and GitHub reconciliation resume work after a crash
          without opening a second PR.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="closing">
      The request you approve names its head commit, the reviewer's verdict, the
      check runs and the preview URL. If any of them change, factory-kit asks
      you again.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

